<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emerita Augusta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">25 BCE–</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Mérida (Roman)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emerita Augusta, modern Mérida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was a Roman colony founded in 25 BCE for veterans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a>. It became the capital of Lusitania and later the seat of the vicarius of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of Hispania</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, amphitheatre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and a long bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> that still survive. The Suebi and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> held it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta.jpg" alt="Emerita Augusta" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamín Núñez González · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_M%C3%A9rida,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2017_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
